  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "execute-controls",
      "text": "With a fresh breaker and default settings, Execute increments Requests before running an admitted function. A nil error counts as a success and clears the failure streak. A non-nil error counts as a failure and clears the success streak. The sixth consecutive failure opens the breaker and clears all counters. Each admitted call still returns its original result and error. A subsequent call while the open timeout has yet to expire returns nil and ErrOpenState without running the function or incrementing Requests. A custom IsSuccessful can change how a returned error is counted while Execute still returns that error.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 243,
        "fragment": "cb.afterRequest(generation, cb.isSuccessful(err))"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "execute-panic-and-cancellation",
      "text": "After Execute admits a closed-state request, a recoverable panic with a non-nil value triggers a failure report and then re-panics with the same value, provided failure reporting returns normally. The report affects counters only if the admission generation is still current. The recovery defer also covers IsSuccessful, so a panic there after a normal request return takes the same path. If the function returns a context cancellation error, the default classifier counts it as a failure. If the function instead calls runtime.Goexit, the defer runs with recover returning nil, so neither outcome is reported and the admission remains counted until the generation clears.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 236,
        "fragment": "if e != nil {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Is panic accounting intended to cover IsSuccessful as well as the request function described by the Execute comment?"
    },
    {
      "id": "lazy-expiry-and-late-results",
      "text": "With a positive closed Interval, an admitted request whose outcome is processed before the deadline is counted in its original generation, assuming no intervening state change. If the generation is cleared first, the same outcome is discarded. If the outcome is the first operation to check time strictly after the deadline, it clears the counters itself and then discards its own result. Counts alone can still return the old counters after the deadline. Likewise, an open breaker does not become half-open from elapsed time alone. The first State, admission, or completion check strictly after the open deadline performs that transition, even if the completion is then discarded as stale.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 298,
        "fragment": "state, generation := cb.currentState(now)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the interval and timeout documentation explain that expiry is applied lazily and that Counts can return counters from an expired generation?"
    },
    {
      "id": "repeated-reports-and-order",
      "text": "With a closed breaker whose generation stays current, one successful Allow followed by two done(true) calls produces one request and two successes. With a half-open breaker and MaxRequests set to 2, admit requests A and B in the same generation. If A reports success twice before B reports failure, the second success closes the breaker and clears the counters, then B's failure is discarded. If B reports failure before A's second success, the failure reopens the breaker and clears the counters, then A's second success is discarded. Each callback reports against its captured generation without tracking whether that particular admission already reported.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 272,
        "fragment": "tscb.cb.afterRequest(generation, success)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Must callers invoke done exactly once, or should repeated invocations be ignored?"
    },
    {
      "id": "abandoned-half-open-admission",
      "text": "After the open timeout, with MaxRequests set to 0 and therefore defaulting to 1, the first Allow moves the breaker to half-open and consumes the only admission. If the caller cancels or abandons the request without invoking done, subsequent Allow calls return a nil callback and ErrTooManyRequests, even after another timeout period. Half-open has no expiry. The original callback can still close the breaker with true or reopen it with false while its generation remains current.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 378,
        "fragment": "cb.expiry = zero"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: How should callers report an admitted request that is canceled or abandoned, given that omission keeps its half-open admission occupied and reporting false reopens the breaker?"
    },
    {
      "id": "state-change-callback-reentry",
      "text": "With OnStateChange configured, when an operation changes state, it stores the new state and clears the counters before calling the hook synchronously under the breaker mutex. If the hook calls State or Counts on that same breaker, the nested call waits for the same mutex and the triggering operation cannot finish. Calling Name from the hook does not acquire the mutex.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 359,
        "fragment": "cb.onStateChange(cb.name, prev, state)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should OnStateChange support querying the same breaker, or should its documentation exclude methods that acquire the breaker mutex?"
    }
  ]
}
